From 0253a4e0dad872336a1ed9507480d4868493669e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SN2 Dev <sn2-dev@local>
Date: Fri, 24 Apr 2026 02:28:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] ia64: implement HAVE_ARCH_JUMP_LABEL

Implements static-key jump-label patching for IA-64.  Each static
branch site occupies one 16-byte MIB-template bundle; the 'off'
state is nop.b 0 in slot 2 and the 'on' state is br.cond.sptk.many
to the target, also in slot 2.  The bundle template is fixed across
both forms, so transforms rewrite only the 41 bits of slot 2, which
live entirely in the high qword of the bundle.  This is a single
8-byte store plus the standard fc / sync.i / srlz.i icache sync that
the existing self-modifying-code paths in arch/ia64/kernel/patch.c
already use (ia64_patch_vtop, ia64_patch_rse).

The inline asm in arch_static_branch() / arch_static_branch_jump()
emits the instruction inside an explicit { ... ;; } bundle group so
the assembler places the nop.b or br.cond in slot 2 of an MIB bundle
whose slots 0 and 1 are nop.m and nop.i.  This guarantees that the
site is exactly one bundle, 16-byte aligned, and independent of
surrounding code scheduling.

arch_jump_label_transform() encodes br.cond.sptk.many from scratch
using the B1 form (op=0x4, wh=sptk, btype=cond) with a signed
21-bit IP-relative displacement expressed in bundles.  The
addressable range is +/- 16 MiB around each bundle, which is
sufficient for all in-kernel static branch sites today.  Out-of-range
targets WARN and leave the bundle unmodified (callers will fall
through to the no-op path in that case).

The local CPU's fc + sync.i + srlz.i is followed by an smp_call_
function IPI that makes every other CPU issue sync.i + srlz.i, so
any stale I-cache copy of the old bundle is discarded before the
next fetch.  For the common case in this tree (sn2_platform_key,
flipped once during ia64_platform_detect() before secondary CPUs
boot) the IPI is effectively a no-op, but it is correct in the
general case and costs nothing when no other CPUs are online.

struct jump_entry is the non-relative 3-u64 form (code/target/key
as absolute virtual addresses).  IA-64 has no KASLR and no FDPIC,
so absolute entries are the simplest correct choice.
HAVE_ARCH_JUMP_LABEL_RELATIVE can be added later as an optimization
that halves the __jump_table size.

  * arch/ia64/include/asm/jump_label.h (new): arch_static_branch,
    arch_static_branch_jump, JUMP_LABEL_NOP_SIZE, struct jump_entry.
  * arch/ia64/kernel/jump_label.c (new): arch_jump_label_transform,
    arch_jump_label_transform_queue/apply, B1 branch encoder.
  * arch/ia64/Kconfig: select HAVE_ARCH_JUMP_LABEL.
  * arch/ia64/kernel/Makefile: build jump_label.o under CONFIG_JUMP_LABEL.

With this in place, the sn2_platform_key runtime dispatch added in
the earlier commits of this series drops from a load+test+branch on
every hot-path call to a patched nop/br.cond pair.
